\section{One local repair}{

OECD's codelist does not describe its own reported aggregates exactly. In
the published data \code{INC_X} ("countries unallocated by income") includes
\code{DPGC_X} ("developing countries unspecified"); in the codelist, \code{DPGC_X} is
not among \code{INC_X}'s children.

The edge \code{INC_X -> DPGC_X} is therefore added here to match the data. It is
not cosmetic: without it, cutting an income classification at country level
descends \code{INC_X} through the codelist and drops \code{DPGC_X} entirely. For the
United States in 2022 that is 7,766.8 million — 32\% of the donor's total —
and the country level would silently fail to match the tier level above it.
The shortfall was measured as exactly \code{DPGC_X}'s value.

The edge creates no double count: \code{DPGC_X} is the geographic residual, and
an income scheme's only route to it is through \code{INC_X}.

\code{data-raw/crs_recipient_tree.R} reports if OECD ever adds the edge itself,
at which point the repair becomes redundant and should be removed.
}

